;; Docs for std/fmt.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "fmt.bjodoc"). The texts
;; are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Formatting in two layers: interpolation of scalars, and a composable layout of blocks.")
  (see println* show render->string with-fmt Block)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std fmt))

    ;; Layer 1: interpolation
    (println* ["Hello, " 42 ", ok? " #t])

    ;; Layer 2: layout
    (show ["Padding: [" (padded/left 10 ["foo"]) "]\n"])
    (show ["Number: " (numeric/comma 1234567.89) "\n"])
    (show ["Columns:\n"
           (columnar [["first long text string" "second text string"]
                      ["col 2" "more text for col 2"]])])
    """)
  (example """
    ;; Strings, ints and chars in a vec literal are blocks, variables too.
    ;; A number bound by def needs its type, or it is not known to be an int.
    (def name "Ada")
    (: n int)
    (def n 3)
    (show ["Hello, " name "! You have " n " new messages" #\. nl])
    ;; Hello, Ada! You have 3 new messages.

    ;; A combinator takes its content in brackets and is one block itself.
    (show [(padded/left 8 ["x = " n]) "|" nl])
    ;;    x = 3|
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A block made from a record, by a function with a signature.
    ;; A double goes through numeric; an if picks a block or nothing.
    (type (: Item (Record (: name string) (: price double) (: stock int))))

    (: item-line (-> Item Block))
    (defun (item-line i)
      (each [(padded/right 10 [(record-ref i name)])
             (padded/left 9 [(numeric (record-ref i price) #:precision 2 #:comma 3)])
             (if (= (record-ref i stock) 0) "  sold out" nothing)
             nl]))

    ;; A vec of blocks is one block with each.
    (show [(each (vec-map item-line stock))])
    ;; Green tea      3.50
    ;; Gaiwan     1,240.00
    ;; Kettle        89.90  sold out
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A row per record. The function says it returns (Vec Block), so the
    ;; literal in it is read as blocks.
    (: item-row (-> Item (Vec Block)))
    (defun (item-row i)
      [(record-ref i name)
       (numeric (record-ref i price) #:precision 2)
       (record-ref i stock)])

    (show [(tabular/rows (vec-map item-row stock)
                         #:titles ["Item" "Price" "Stock"]
                         #:align [AlignLeft (AlignOn #\.) AlignRight]
                         #:style StyleAscii)])
    ;; +-----------+---------+-------+
    ;; | Item      |   Price | Stock |
    ;; +-----------+---------+-------+
    ;; | Green tea |    3.50 |    12 |
    ;; | Gaiwan    | 1240.00 |     2 |
    ;; | Kettle    |   89.90 |     0 |
    ;; +-----------+---------+-------+
    """)
  (example """
    ;; In a vec literal, what a match gives is read as any element is: the
    ;; string, or a block. A function that returns one block puts its
    ;; pieces under each.
    (show ["Title: " (match title ((Some t) t) (None "-")) nl])

    (: greeting (-> string (Option string) Block))
    (defun (greeting name title)
      (each ["Hello, "
             (match title
               ((Some t) (each [t " "]))
               (None nothing))
             name "!"]))

    (show [(greeting "Ada" (Some "Dr")) nl (greeting "Bob" None) nl])
    ;; Hello, Dr Ada!
    ;; Hello, Bob!
    """)
  (reference """
    @strong{Interpolation} combines scalars of mixed types into text. It
    works on a @code{(Vec Disp)}: a vec literal whose elements are strings,
    ints, doubles, chars and bools. The grammar @code{Disp} reads each element
    into the case that holds it. @code{println*} prints one.

    @strong{Layout} is a block algebra, for padding, aligning, wrapping
    paragraphs and laying out tables. The unit is a @code{Block}: a string,
    int or char, which needs no wrapping in a vec literal, or a @code{BRun},
    a function that writes to an output sink and threads the current column
    through. Every combinator takes its content as a @code{(Vec Block)} and
    returns a @code{Block}, allowing them to nest. Nothing is written until
    the blocks are run by @code{show}, @code{render} or @code{render->string}.

    @strong{A block and a vec of blocks.} Two types do all the work, and a
    type error in a layout is nearly always one of them where the other is
    wanted:

    @read-list{
     - A @code{Block} is one piece of output: a string, an int, a char, or a combinator such as @code{(padded/left 5 ["x"])}.
     - A @code{(Vec Block)} is pieces of output in order, written as a vec literal: @code{["x = " 7 nl]}.
    }

    Every combinator takes its content as a @code{(Vec Block)} and gives
    back one @code{Block}. @code{show}, @code{render} and
    @code{render->string} take a @code{(Vec Block)}. So the brackets go
    around content, and a combinator call is one element in them:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (show (padded/left 5 "x"))       ; refused: "x" is not content, and show wants a vec
    (show [(padded/left 5 ["x"])])   ; "    x"
    (show [(padded/left 5 ["x"]) (padded/left 5 ["y"]) nl])   ; two blocks, then a newline
    }|

    More brackets do not help. @code{[["x"]]} is a vec in a vec, and a vec
    is not a block. The one place where vecs of vecs are right is a table:
    @code{tabular} takes a vec of columns and @code{tabular/rows} a vec of
    rows, each of them a @code{(Vec Block)}.

    @strong{What a vec literal can hold.} A vec literal is read element by
    element where a @code{(Vec Block)} is wanted: as an argument of a
    function that takes one, or as the value of a binding whose type is
    written. Each element is one of these:

    @read-table{
    | Element                                                        | Is read as                         |
    |----------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------|
    | A string, an int or a char: a literal, a variable or a call     | @code{BStr}, @code{BInt}, @code{BChar} |
    | A @code{Block}: @code{nl}, @code{nothing}, a combinator call, a variable of type @code{Block} | itself |
    | An @code{if}, @code{cond}, @code{match} or @code{let} whose results are any of these | the result it gives |
    }

    Anything else is converted first:

    @read-table{
    | You have                          | Write                                                     |
    |-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------|
    | A double @code{x}                 | @code{(numeric x)}, or @code{(numeric x #:precision 2)}   |
    | A bool @code{b}                   | @code{(if b "yes" "no")}                                  |
    | A long @code{n}                   | @code{(long->string n)}, or @code{(bytes n)} for a size   |
    | A @code{(Vec Block)} @code{bs}    | @code{(each bs)}                                          |
    | A vec literal                     | @code{(each [...])}, or its elements in the outer vec     |
    | A @code{(Vec string)} @code{xs}   | @code{(each (vec-map BStr xs))}                           |
    | A @code{(List %a)} @code{xs}      | @code{(joined f xs [", "])}, where @code{f} makes the block of one element |
    }

    Nothing is converted outside such a vec literal. A function declared
    to return a @code{Block} returns @code{(BStr s)} or @code{(each [s])},
    not @code{s}. A vec literal of mixed elements in a lambda, such as one
    given to @code{vec-map}, has no type to be read at, and is refused: put
    it under @code{each}, or make the lambda a function with a signature.
    A function that takes blocks needs its signature for the same reason.

    @strong{A number bound by @code{def}.} @code{(def n 3)} does not say
    what kind of number @code{n} is, so @code{[n]} cannot read it as a
    block, and the compiler says so at @code{n}. Write @code{(: n int)}
    above it. The same holds for
    @code{(def xs [1 2 3])} and @code{(def total (+ 1 2))}. A number
    written in the vec itself, as in @code{["n = " 3]}, is an int.

    @strong{When it does not compile.} The messages for the common mistakes,
    and what to write instead:

    @read-table{
    | The compiler says                                                  | Write                                              |
    |--------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------|
    | expected @code{(Vec fmt/Block)}, found @code{string}                | @code{["x"]} for @code{"x"}                        |
    | expected @code{(Vec fmt/Block)}, found @code{fmt/Block}             | @code{[b]} for @code{b}                            |
    | expected @code{fmt/Block}, found @code{(Vec ...)}                   | @code{(each bs)} for @code{bs}                     |
    | They differ inside the Vec: @code{fmt/Block} against @code{string}  | @code{(: bs (Vec Block))} above @code{(def bs ...)} |
    | Block: expected a string, an int, a char ...; found a vec            | @code{(each [...])}, or no inner brackets          |
    | Block: ... found the number 1.5                                      | @code{(numeric 1.5)}                               |
    | Block: ... found a boolean                                           | @code{(if b "yes" "no")}                           |
    | Block: 'n' is a number, and nothing says yet whether it is an int ... | @code{(: n int)} above @code{(def n 3)}           |
    | what @code{f} returns has the wrong type: expected @code{fmt/Block}, found @code{string} | @code{(BStr s)} or @code{(each [s])} |
    }

    A combinator of your own is a @code{BRun} and the two functions that run
    blocks, @code{run-block} and @code{run-blocks}. It is the same kind of
    thing as the ones in this module:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (: boxed (-> (Vec Block) Block))
    (defun (boxed bs)
      (BRun (fun (out col)
              (run-blocks out col ["[" (render->string bs) "]"]))))
    }|

    @strong{Settings} are parameters, so they are dynamically scoped:
    @code{with-fmt} binds one around some blocks while they run, and it is
    restored when they end. The ones here are:

    @read-table{
    | Parameter                     | Default                | What it sets                                            |
    |-------------------------------+------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------|
    | @code{current-pad}            | @code{#\space}         | The character padding is made of.                       |
    | @code{current-width}          | @code{78}              | The right margin of @code{wrapped}, @code{justified}, @code{columnar}, and a table that cuts or wraps. |
    | @code{current-ellipsis}       | @code{""}              | What replaces the part a trim cuts off.                 |
    | @code{current-string-width}   | the character count    | How wide a string is, in columns. @code{terminal-width} counts as a terminal does. |
    | @code{current-precision}      | @code{-1}              | Digits after the decimal separator; negative is as many as needed. |
    | @code{current-comma}          | @code{0}               | Digits per group; zero is no grouping.                  |
    | @code{current-comma-sep}      | @code{#\,}             | The character between groups.                           |
    | @code{current-decimal-sep}    | @code{#\.}             | The decimal separator.                                  |
    | @code{current-signed}         | @code{#f}              | Whether a positive number gets a leading @code{+}.      |
    }

    A setting of your own works the same way: @code{with-fmt} takes the
    parameter as an argument. Read it with @code{parameter-ref} inside the
    @code{BRun}'s function, when the block runs. Read where the block is
    built, it is read before any enclosing @code{with-fmt} has bound it.

    @strong{Tables} are @code{tabular}, @code{tabular/rows} and
    @code{columnar} as functions, @code{table-row} a row at a time, and
    @code{Table} as a quoted literal that the compiler checks. They draw
    borders in a @code{TableStyle}, a header and rules, align a column on a
    character, and fit @code{current-width} by cutting or wrapping cells.
    @code{bytes}, @code{percent}, @code{bar} and @code{sparkline} are blocks
    for what goes in a cell, or anywhere else. Set
    @code{current-string-width} to @code{terminal-width} for text with wide
    characters or colours in it.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Types
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Disp
  (summary "A scalar to interpolate: a string, int, double, char or bool.")
  (case DStr "A string, as it is.")
  (case DInt "An int, by int->string.")
  (case DDouble "A double, by double->string.")
  (case DChar "A char, as one character.")
  (case DBool "A bool, as #t or #f.")
  (see println*)
  (reference """
    @code{Disp} is a grammar (see @code{def/grammar}). In a vec literal of
    mixed scalars, such as @code{["n = " 42 ", ok? " #t]}, the grammar reads
    each element into the case that can hold it. Thus you rarely write the
    cases.
    """))

(:doc Sink
  (summary "Where a block writes its text: a function given each piece of it.")
  (see Block run-block))

(:doc Block
  (summary "A piece of layout: a scalar, or a function that writes output and returns the final column position.")
  (case BStr "A string, written as it is.")
  (case BInt "An int, written in decimal.")
  (case BChar "A char.")
  (case BRun "A function taking (sink, col) that writes output and returns the resulting column.")
  (see run-block each captured)
  (example """
    (render->string ["x = " 7 #\!])   ; "x = 7!": the leaves need no wrapping
    """)
  (example """
    ;; From one type to the other.
    (def s "tea")
    (: b Block)
    (def b (BStr s))               ; a string as a block, outside a vec literal
    (: bs (Vec Block))
    (def bs [s " and " b])         ; blocks in order: the type is written, so the literal is read
    (show [(each bs) nl])          ; tea and tea: a vec of blocks as one block
    (show [(each (vec-map BStr ["a" "b"])) nl])   ; ab: a vec of strings
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A function that returns one block, and one that returns content.
    (: bullets (-> (Vec string) Block))
    (defun (bullets xs)
      (each (vec-map (fun (x) (each ["  * " x nl])) xs)))

    (: labelled (-> string int (Vec Block)))
    (defun (labelled label n)
      [label ": " n nl])

    (show [(bullets ["tea" "milk"]) (each (labelled "cups" 3))])
    (show (labelled "pots" 1))
    ;;   * tea
    ;;   * milk
    ;; cups: 3
    ;; pots: 1
    """)
  (reference """
    The column is counted in characters since the last newline written. It
    is the one thing threaded through every block, and what lets @code{fl},
    @code{space-to} and @code{wrapped} know where the cursor is.

    @code{Block} is a grammar (see @code{def/grammar}). In a vec literal, a
    string, an int or a char is a block, and a value of type @code{Block},
    such as @code{nl}, goes in as it is. This happens only where the literal
    is wanted as a @code{(Vec Block)}; elsewhere, write @code{BStr},
    @code{BInt} or @code{BChar}. The module doc lists what else goes in a
    vec literal of blocks and how to convert what does not.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Layer 1: interpolation
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc println*
  (summary "Prints scalars of mixed types, then a newline.")
  (arg ds "The scalars, written as a vec literal.")
  (see Disp show)
  (example """
    (println* ["n = " 42 ", ok? " #t ", " #\x ", " 1.5])
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Running blocks
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc render
  (summary "Writes blocks to a port.")
  (arg port "The port to write to.")
  (arg bs "The blocks, run from column 0.")
  (see show render->string)
  (reference """
    It is @code{#:sync}, and cannot be otherwise: the sink is stored in a
    @code{BRun}, and a stored function's colour is its type, so no
    suspending sink can go there. To render from a fiber, use
    @code{render->string} and write the string yourself.
    """))

(:doc show
  (summary "Writes blocks to the current output port.")
  (arg bs "The blocks, run from column 0.")
  (see render render->string)
  (example """
    (show ["Padding: [" (padded/left 10 ["foo"]) "]\n"])
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{render} to @code{current-output-port}, and @code{#:sync} for the
    same reason.
    """))

(:doc render->string
  (summary "The text blocks write, as a string.")
  (arg bs "The blocks, run from column 0.")
  (returns "Everything the blocks wrote.")
  (see show render captured)
  (example """
    (render->string [(padded/left 5 ["7"])])   ; "    7"
    """))

(:doc run-block
  (summary "Runs one block: the engine's step, for writing combinators.")
  (arg out "The sink to write to.")
  (arg col "The column the block starts at.")
  (arg b "The block.")
  (returns "The column it ends at.")
  (see run-blocks Block))

(:doc run-blocks
  (summary "Runs blocks one after another, threading the column.")
  (arg out "The sink to write to.")
  (arg col "The column the first block starts at.")
  (arg bs "The blocks.")
  (returns "The column the last one ends at.")
  (see run-block))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Settings
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc with-fmt
  (summary "Runs blocks with a parameter bound to a value.")
  (tparam %a "The parameter's type.")
  (arg p "The parameter: one of this module's settings, or one of your own.")
  (arg v "Its value while the blocks run.")
  (arg bs "The blocks.")
  (returns "A block. The parameter is bound when it runs, not when it is built, and restored when it ends.")
  (see with-pad current-width)
  (example """
    (show [(with-fmt current-precision 2 [(numeric 3.14159)])])   ; 3.14
    """)
  (example """
    ;; Settings nest; each applies to the blocks inside it.
    (show [(with-fmt current-width 24
             [(with-fmt current-ellipsis "…"
                [(trimmed/right 8 ["A green tea from the hills"]) nl])
              (wrapped ["A green tea from the hills, picked in spring and rolled by hand."])])
           nl])
    ;; A green…
    ;; A green tea from the
    ;; hills, picked in spring
    ;; and rolled by hand.
    """))

(:doc with-pad
  (summary "Runs blocks with another padding character.")
  (arg c "The padding character.")
  (arg bs "The blocks.")
  (returns "A block: @code{(with-fmt current-pad c bs)}.")
  (see current-pad space-to)
  (example """
    (show [(with-pad #\. ["ab" (space-to 6) "c"])])   ; ab....c, a dot leader
    """))

(:doc current-pad
  (summary "The character padding is made of. A space unless set.")
  (see with-pad padded/left space-to)
  (reference """
    Padding that is not a whole number of pad characters wide is as many as
    fit. Words in @code{wrapped} and @code{justified} are separated by it
    too.
    """))

(:doc current-width
  (summary "The right margin: what wrapped, justified and columnar fill up to. 78 unless set.")
  (see wrapped justified columnar))

(:doc current-ellipsis
  (summary "What replaces the part a trim cuts off. Empty, which cuts silently, unless set.")
  (see trimmed/right)
  (example """
    (with-fmt current-ellipsis "..." [(trimmed/right 6 ["abcdefgh"])])   ; abc...
    """)
  (reference """
    It takes its room out of the content, so a trimmed text is never wider
    than asked for. An ellipsis wider than that is cut itself.
    """))

(:doc current-string-width
  (summary "How many columns a string takes. Its character count unless set.")
  (see width-of)
  (example """
    ;; An astral character takes two columns on a terminal.
    (defun (terminal-width s)
      (string-fold (fun (ch acc) (+ acc (if (> (char->integer ch) 65535) 2 1))) 0 s))

    (with-fmt current-string-width terminal-width [(padded/left 5 ["😀"])])
    """)
  (reference """
    Everything that measures or cuts goes through it: padding, trimming,
    wrapping and tables. The column a @code{BRun} is handed is not: it
    counts characters.
    """))

(:doc terminal-width
  (summary "How many columns a string takes in a terminal, for current-string-width.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "Its width in columns.")
  (see current-string-width width-of)
  (example """
    (def esc (char->string (integer->char 27)))
    (terminal-width "tea")                                   ;; 3
    (terminal-width "茶")                                     ;; 2
    (terminal-width (str "e" (char->string (integer->char 769))))  ;; 1: e and an accent
    (terminal-width (str esc "[1mtea" esc "[0m"))             ;; 3: the escapes take none

    (with-fmt current-string-width terminal-width
      [(tabular/rows [["茶" "1"] ["tea" "2"]] #:style StyleAscii)])
    """)
  (reference """
    East Asian Wide and Fullwidth characters, emoji among them, take two
    columns. Combining marks, format and control characters take none. An
    escape sequence takes none: a CSI, as in a colour, ends at a character
    from @code{@"@"} to @code{~}, and an OSC, as in a link, at BEL or at ESC and a
    backslash. What follows a zero-width joiner takes no columns of its own,
    so a family emoji is two wide. Variation selector 16 makes a one-column
    character before it two wide, a skin tone modifier joins the emoji
    before it, and two regional indicators are one flag, two wide.

    It is not the default measure, which counts code points and is
    cheaper. Set it where the text is for a terminal and holds such
    characters: a wide character measured as one column puts every column
    after it out of line.

    The table is Unicode 16.0. A terminal whose own idea of a character's
    width differs from it, as some do for emoji, draws that character wider
    or narrower than measured.
    """))

(:doc current-precision
  (summary "Digits after the decimal separator. Negative, the default, is as many as the number needs.")
  (see numeric)
  (reference """
    As many as it needs is what @code{double->string} would give. A
    precision rounds.
    """))

(:doc current-comma
  (summary "Digits per group in the integer part. Zero, the default, is no grouping.")
  (see numeric numeric/comma current-comma-sep))

(:doc current-comma-sep
  (summary "The character between groups of digits. A comma unless set.")
  (see current-comma numeric))

(:doc current-decimal-sep
  (summary "The decimal separator. A full stop unless set.")
  (see numeric current-comma-sep))

(:doc current-signed
  (summary "Whether a number with no sign of its own gets a leading +. #f unless set.")
  (see numeric))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The block algebra
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc nothing
  (summary "An empty block that writes nothing, suitable as a no-op branch in conditionals.")
  (see each)
  (example """
    (render->string ["a" (if verbose? nl nothing) "b"])
    """)
  (example """
    ;; The arm of a match that writes nothing.
    (show ["Hello, "
           (match title
             ((Some t) (each [t " "]))
             (None nothing))
           name nl])
    """))

(:doc each
  (summary "Many blocks as one.")
  (arg bs "The blocks.")
  (returns "A block that runs them in order.")
  (see nothing joined Block)
  (example """
    ;; Content that is already a vec, where one block is wanted.
    (: parts (Vec Block))
    (def parts ["a" 1 #\b])
    (show ["<" (each parts) ">" nl])          ; <a1b>
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A block per element of a vec. The literal is under each, so it is
    ;; read as blocks; a bare literal in the lambda would not be.
    (show [(each (vec-map (fun (i) (each [(record-ref i name) ": " (record-ref i stock) nl]))
                          stock))])
    ;; Green tea: 12
    ;; Gaiwan: 2
    ;; Kettle: 0
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A function that returns one block of several pieces.
    (: price-tag (-> string double Block))
    (defun (price-tag name price)
      (each [name " (" (numeric price #:precision 2) ")"]))
    """)
  (reference """
    It is how a @code{(Vec Block)} goes where a @code{Block} is wanted: in
    a vec literal, as the value of a function that returns a block, or as
    a cell of a table. It costs nothing to nest.
    """))

(:doc captured
  (summary "Renders blocks to a string and passes it to a function returning a replacement block.")
  (arg f "Given the text the blocks wrote; its block is run in their place.")
  (arg bs "The blocks to render.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see render->string)
  (example """
    (captured (fun (s) (BInt (string-count s))) ["abcd"])   ; writes 4
    """)
  (reference """
    How a combinator that needs the whole text, rather than writing as it
    goes, is made: padding, trimming and wrapping are all written with it.
    The blocks are rendered from column 0, and @code{f}'s block runs at the
    current column.
    """))

(:doc width-of
  (summary "How many columns a string takes, by current-string-width.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "Its width.")
  (see current-string-width))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Vertical and horizontal space
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc nl
  (summary "A newline.")
  (see fl))

(:doc fl
  (summary "A fresh line: a newline, unless the cursor is already at the start of one.")
  (see nl)
  (example """
    (render->string ["a" nl fl "b"])   ; "a\nb"
    (render->string [fl "a"])          ; "a"
    """))

(:doc space-to
  (summary "Pads to a column.")
  (arg goal "The column to move to.")
  (returns "A block that writes padding up to goal, and nothing if the cursor is already at or past it.")
  (see tab-to with-pad)
  (example """
    (render->string ["ab" (space-to 5) "c"])   ; "ab   c"
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A dot leader: every price starts at column 12.
    (show [(with-pad #\. ["Tea" (space-to 12) "3.50"]) nl
           (with-pad #\. ["Gaiwan" (space-to 12) "1240.00"]) nl])
    ;; Tea.........3.50
    ;; Gaiwan......1240.00
    """)
  (reference """
    It fills with @code{current-pad}, so under @code{with-pad} it is a
    leader. It never moves backwards.
    """))

(:doc tab-to
  (summary "Pads to the next tab stop.")
  (key width "The distance between tab stops. 8 unless given.")
  (returns "A block that pads to the next multiple of width.")
  (see space-to)
  (example """
    (render->string ["ab" (tab-to #:width 4) "c"])     ; "ab  c"
    (render->string ["abcd" (tab-to #:width 4) "c"])   ; "abcd    c"
    """)
  (reference """
    It always moves: at a tab stop already, it pads to the next one.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Fitting to a width
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc padded/left
  (summary "Pads content on the left to a width: aligned right.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block. Content wider than w is left as it is: padding never cuts.")
  (see padded/right padded/both fitted/left current-pad)
  (example """
    (render->string [(padded/left 6 ["x=" 7])])   ; "   x=7"
    """)
  (example """
    ;; The content is a vec even when it is one thing.
    (show [(padded/left 9 [(numeric 1240.0 #:precision 2 #:comma 3)]) nl])   ;  1,240.00
    (show [(with-pad #\0 [(padded/left 4 [42])]) nl])                        ; 0042
    """))

(:doc padded/right
  (summary "Pads content on the right to a width: aligned left.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block. Content wider than w is left as it is.")
  (see padded/left padded/both fitted/right)
  (example """
    (show [(padded/right 10 ["Green tea"]) "3.50" nl
           (padded/right 10 ["Gaiwan"]) "1240.00" nl])
    ;; Green tea 3.50
    ;; Gaiwan    1240.00
    """))

(:doc padded/both
  (summary "Pads content on both sides to a width: centred.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block, with the odd column, if there is one, on the right. Content wider than w is left as it is.")
  (see padded/left padded/right fitted/both)
  (example """
    (show [(with-pad #\= [(padded/both 20 [" Receipt "])]) nl])
    ;; ===== Receipt ======
    """))

(:doc trimmed/left
  (summary "Cuts content to a width, keeping its end.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block. Content that fits is left as it is; otherwise current-ellipsis stands at the start, where the cut was.")
  (see trimmed/right trimmed/both fitted/left current-ellipsis)
  (example """
    (render->string [(trimmed/left 3 ["abcdef"])])   ; "def"
    """))

(:doc trimmed/right
  (summary "Cuts content to a width, keeping its start.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block. Content that fits is left as it is; otherwise current-ellipsis stands at the end.")
  (see trimmed/left trimmed/both fitted/right current-ellipsis)
  (example """
    (render->string [(trimmed/right 3 ["abcdef"])])   ; "abc"
    """))

(:doc trimmed/both
  (summary "Cuts content to a width, keeping its middle.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block. Content that fits is left as it is; otherwise current-ellipsis stands at both ends.")
  (see trimmed/left trimmed/right fitted/both)
  (example """
    (render->string [(trimmed/both 4 ["abcdefgh"])])   ; "cdef"
    """))

(:doc fitted/left
  (summary "Content at exactly a width, aligned right: padded if short, cut if long.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block: trimmed/left, then padded/left.")
  (see padded/left trimmed/left))

(:doc fitted/right
  (summary "Content at exactly a width, aligned left: padded if short, cut if long.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block: trimmed/right, then padded/right.")
  (see padded/right trimmed/right)
  (example """
    (render->string [(fitted/right 5 ["ab"]) "|"])       ; "ab   |"
    (render->string [(fitted/right 3 ["abcdef"]) "|"])   ; "abc|"
    """))

(:doc fitted/both
  (summary "Content at exactly a width, centred: padded if short, cut in the middle if long.")
  (arg w "The width, in columns.")
  (arg bs "The content.")
  (returns "A block: trimmed/both, then padded/both.")
  (see padded/both trimmed/both)
  (example """
    (show ["[" (fitted/both 6 ["ab"]) "]" nl])           ; [  ab  ]
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Sequences
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc joined
  (summary "Every element of a list, formatted, with a separator between them.")
  (tparam %a "The elements' type.")
  (arg f "Formats one element.")
  (arg xs "The elements.")
  (arg sep "The separator, itself blocks.")
  (returns "A block. One element has no separator, and none is nothing.")
  (see joined/prefix joined/suffix joined/last)
  (example """
    (show [(joined (fun (n) (BInt n)) (list 1 2 3) [", "])])   ; 1, 2, 3
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A constructor of Block is a function, so a list of strings is
    ;; joined with BStr.
    (show [(joined BStr (list "tea" "milk" "honey") [", "]) nl])   ; tea, milk, honey

    ;; f returns a block, so content of several pieces is under each.
    (show [(joined (fun (s) (each ["* " s])) (list "tea" "milk") [nl]) nl])
    ;; * tea
    ;; * milk

    ;; For a vec, a separator is vec->list away.
    (show [(joined BStr (vec->list ["a" "b"]) [" | "]) nl])        ; a | b
    """))

(:doc joined/prefix
  (summary "Every element of a list, formatted, with a separator before each, the first included.")
  (tparam %a "The elements' type.")
  (arg f "Formats one element.")
  (arg xs "The elements.")
  (arg sep "The separator, itself blocks.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see joined joined/suffix)
  (example """
    (show [(joined/prefix (fun (s) (BStr s)) (list "a" "b") [","])])   ; ,a,b
    """))

(:doc joined/suffix
  (summary "Every element of a list, formatted, with a separator after each, the last included.")
  (tparam %a "The elements' type.")
  (arg f "Formats one element.")
  (arg xs "The elements.")
  (arg sep "The separator, itself blocks.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see joined joined/prefix)
  (example """
    (show [(joined/suffix (fun (s) (BStr s)) (list "a" "b") [","])])   ; a,b,
    """))

(:doc joined/last
  (summary "Like joined, with the last element formatted by a function of its own.")
  (tparam %a "The elements' type.")
  (arg f "Formats every element but the last.")
  (arg last-f "Formats the last element.")
  (arg xs "The elements.")
  (arg sep "The separator, itself blocks.")
  (returns "A block. A list of one is formatted by last-f alone.")
  (see joined)
  (example """
    (show [(joined/last (fun (s) (BStr s))
                        (fun (s) (each ["and " (BStr s)]))
                        (list "a" "b" "c")
                        [", "])])
    ;; a, b, and c
    """)
  (reference """
    The separator is written before the last element too, so what
    @code{last-f} adds is a word, not a comma.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Numbers
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc numeric
  (summary "A number, with a precision, grouped digits and separators of your choosing.")
  (arg x "The number. An int is formatted as (cast double n).")
  (key precision "Digits after the decimal separator. Negative, the default, is current-precision.")
  (key comma "Digits per group; 0 is no grouping. Negative, the default, is current-comma.")
  (key comma-sep "The character between groups. #\\nul, the default, is current-comma-sep.")
  (key decimal-sep "The decimal separator. #\\nul, the default, is current-decimal-sep.")
  (key signed "#t gives a positive number a leading +. #f, the default, is current-signed.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see numeric/comma current-precision current-comma)
  (example """
    (numeric 2.0 #:precision 2)                  ; 2.00
    (numeric -3.14159 #:precision 3)             ; -3.142
    (numeric 1234567.891 #:precision 2 #:comma 3)  ; 1,234,567.89
    (numeric 42.0 #:signed #t)                   ; +42
    (numeric 1234.5 #:comma 3 #:comma-sep #\space #:decimal-sep #\,)  ; 1 234,5
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A double is not a block. numeric makes it one.
    (: total double)
    (def total 1333.4)
    (show ["Total: " (numeric total #:precision 2 #:comma 3) nl])   ; Total: 1,333.40

    ;; The settings apply to every numeric inside a with-fmt.
    (show [(with-fmt current-precision 1 ["pi is about " (numeric 3.14159) nl])])
    ;; pi is about 3.1
    """)
  (reference """
    The digits are the invariant culture's and the separators are settings,
    so the machine's locale never shows. A keyword not given defaults to its
    parameter when the block runs. Since a keyword's default is what stands
    for the parameter, a keyword cannot ask for that default against a
    parameter bound otherwise: @code{#:signed #f} under a
    @code{current-signed} of @code{#t} still signs, and a negative
    @code{#:precision} still reads @code{current-precision}.
    """))

(:doc numeric/comma
  (summary "A number with its digits grouped in thousands: the common case.")
  (arg x "The number.")
  (returns "A block: @code{(numeric x #:comma 3)}.")
  (see numeric)
  (example """
    (numeric/comma 1234567.0)   ; 1,234,567
    (numeric/comma 42.0)        ; 42
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Paragraphs and tables
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc bytes
  (summary "A number of bytes, in the largest unit there is at least one of.")
  (arg n "The number of bytes.")
  (key si "Units of 1000, kB and MB, rather than of 1024, KiB and MiB. False unless given.")
  (key precision "Digits after the decimal separator, in any unit but B. One unless given.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see percent numeric current-decimal-sep)
  (example """
    (show [(bytes 512L) " " (bytes 1258291L) " " (bytes 1500L #:si #t)])
    ;; 512 B 1.2 MiB 1.5 kB
    """)
  (reference """
    Bytes are whole, so a size under one kibibyte, or kilobyte, has no
    decimals. The units go to EiB, or EB. The decimal separator is
    @code{current-decimal-sep}.
    """))

(:doc percent
  (summary "A share, written as a percentage: 0.125 is 12.5%.")
  (arg x "The share, 1.0 being all of it.")
  (key precision "Digits after the decimal separator. One unless given.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see bytes bar numeric)
  (example """
    (show [(percent 0.125) " " (percent 1.0 #:precision 0)])
    ;; 12.5% 100%
    """))

(:doc bar
  (summary "A horizontal bar, filled to a fraction of its width to an eighth of a column.")
  (arg fraction "How much of the bar is filled, from 0 to 1; a fraction outside that is the nearer end.")
  (key width "The width of the whole bar in columns. Ten unless given.")
  (key empty "What the part not filled is made of, a column at a time. A space unless given.")
  (returns "A block, exactly width columns wide.")
  (see sparkline percent)
  (example """
    (show ["[" (bar 0.62 #:width 20) "]"])
    ;; [████████████▍       ]
    (show ["[" (bar 0.5 #:width 5 #:empty "░") "]"])
    ;; [██▌░░]
    """)
  (reference """
    The filled part is whole blocks, █, and one of the seven eighths of a
    block, ▏ to ▉, for what is left over, rounded to the nearest eighth.
    """))

(:doc sparkline
  (summary "Numbers as a line of bars of eight heights, a column each.")
  (arg xs "The numbers.")
  (returns "A block, a column for each number.")
  (see bar)
  (example """
    (show [(sparkline [1.0 2.0 4.0 8.0 4.0 2.0 1.0])])
    ;; ▁▂▄█▄▂▁
    """)
  (reference """
    The smallest number is the lowest bar, ▁, and the largest the highest,
    █; the rest are divided evenly between them. Numbers that are all the
    same are all in the middle, ▄.
    """))

(:doc wrapped
  (summary "Content filled to the right margin, ragged on the right.")
  (arg bs "The content. Every run of whitespace in it, newlines too, is a break between words.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see justified current-width)
  (example """
    (with-fmt current-width 12 [(wrapped ["the quick brown fox jumps"])])
    ;; the quick
    ;; brown fox
    ;; jumps
    """)
  (example """
    ;; The first line starts after the label, so it has less room; the
    ;; lines after it start at column 0. The content may mix strings and
    ;; numbers like any other.
    (show [(with-fmt current-width 30
             ["Note: " (wrapped ["picked on day " 3 " of spring, rolled by hand and dried in the sun."])])
           nl])
    ;; Note: picked on day 3 of
    ;; spring, rolled by hand and
    ;; dried in the sun.
    """)
  (reference """
    The first line starts wherever the cursor is, and has only the room left
    before @code{current-width}. Words are separated by one
    @code{current-pad}. A word wider than a line gets a line of its own
    rather than being cut.
    """))

(:doc justified
  (summary "Content filled to the right margin and flush against both, the last line excepted.")
  (arg bs "The content. Every run of whitespace in it is a break between words.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see wrapped current-width)
  (example """
    (with-fmt current-width 12 [(justified ["the quick brown fox jumps"])])
    ;; the    quick
    ;; brown    fox
    ;; jumps
    """)
  (reference """
    Lines are broken as @code{wrapped} breaks them, then the gaps are
    widened to fill the line, the leftmost ones taking any extra column. The
    last line, and a line of one word, are not stretched.
    """))

(:doc columnar
  (summary "Columns side by side, dividing current-width evenly and cutting what does not fit.")
  (arg cols "The columns, each a vec of blocks; a newline in one starts its next row.")
  (key sep "Written between the columns. A space unless given.")
  (key align "The alignment of each column, from the first; a column not given one is AlignLeft.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see tabular Align current-width)
  (example """
    (show [(with-fmt current-width 11
             [(columnar [["abc" nl "def"]
                         ["123" nl "456"]])])])
    ;; abc   123
    ;; def   456
    """)
  (reference """
    Each column is rendered on its own and the lines are laid out together.
    The width left once the separators are taken out is shared equally, and
    every cell is cut and padded to its share, the last column cut but not
    padded on the right. A cut keeps the side the cell is aligned to: the
    start of a left-aligned cell, the end of a right-aligned one, the middle
    of a centred one. A column with fewer lines than the others contributes
    blanks, and blanks at the end of a line are left out, separators and
    all. The table starts on a fresh line, and every row ends with a newline.
    """))

(:doc tabular
  (summary "Columns side by side, each as wide as its widest line, cutting nothing.")
  (arg cols "The columns, each a vec of blocks; a newline in one starts its next row.")
  (key sep "Written between the columns, without borders. A space unless given.")
  (key align "The alignment of each column, from the first; a column not given one is AlignLeft.")
  (key style "How the borders are drawn. StyleNone unless given.")
  (key titles "A header row, a block per column. None unless given.")
  (key rules "Which rules there are inside the borders. RulesHeader unless given.")
  (key widths "How wide each column is, from the first; a column not given a rule is as wide as its content.")
  (key overflow "What a cell wider than its column does. OverflowPush unless given.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see tabular/rows columnar Align)
  (example """
    (show [(tabular [["a" nl "bbb"]
                     ["1" nl "2"]])])
    ;; a   1
    ;; bbb 2

    (show [(tabular [["a" nl "bbb"]
                     ["1" nl "2"]]
                    #:sep " | ")])
    ;; a   | 1
    ;; bbb | 2
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A vec of columns, each a vec of blocks, with nl between its cells.
    ;; Here the brackets in brackets are right: a table is a vec of vecs.
    (show [(tabular [["tea" nl "gaiwan" nl "kettle"]
                     [3 nl 1240 nl 90]]
                    #:sep "  " #:align [AlignLeft AlignRight])])
    ;; tea        3
    ;; gaiwan  1240
    ;; kettle    90
    """)
  (reference """
    Laid out as @code{columnar} lays them out, but with each column padded
    to the width its own content asks for, and nothing cut. A left-aligned
    last column is not padded at all. A column with fewer lines than the
    others contributes blanks, and blanks at the end of a line are left out,
    separators and all. The table starts on a fresh line, and every row ends
    with a newline.
    """))

(:doc tabular/rows
  (summary "A table given as rows, each column as wide as its widest cell.")
  (arg rows "The rows, each a vec of cells, a block per cell; a newline in a cell gives its row another line.")
  (key sep "Written between the columns, without borders. A space unless given.")
  (key align "The alignment of each column, from the first; a column not given one is AlignLeft.")
  (key style "How the borders are drawn. StyleNone unless given.")
  (key titles "A header row, a block per column. None unless given.")
  (key rules "Which rules there are inside the borders. RulesHeader unless given.")
  (key widths "How wide each column is, from the first; a column not given a rule is as wide as its content.")
  (key overflow "What a cell wider than its column does. OverflowPush unless given.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see tabular table-row TableStyle)
  (example """
    (show [(tabular/rows [["name" "n" "time"]
                          ["sort" 10 "1.2 µs"]
                          ["sort" 1000 "210 µs"]]
                         #:sep "  "
                         #:align [AlignLeft AlignRight AlignRight])])
    ;; name     n    time
    ;; sort    10  1.2 µs
    ;; sort  1000  210 µs
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A cell is one block. A cell of several pieces is under each, and a
    ;; newline in it gives its row another line.
    (show [(tabular/rows [["tea" (each [3 " cups"])]
                          ["kettle" (each ["boils in" nl "4 minutes"])]]
                         #:style StyleAscii)])
    ;; +--------+-----------+
    ;; | tea    | 3 cups    |
    ;; | kettle | boils in  |
    ;; |        | 4 minutes |
    ;; +--------+-----------+
    """)
  (example """
    ;; Rows made from data. A function with a signature makes each row, so
    ;; that the literal in it is read as blocks.
    (: item-row (-> Item (Vec Block)))
    (defun (item-row i)
      [(record-ref i name) (numeric (record-ref i price) #:precision 2) (record-ref i stock)])

    (show [(tabular/rows (vec-map item-row stock)
                         #:titles ["Item" "Price" "Stock"]
                         #:align [AlignLeft (AlignOn #\.) AlignRight]
                         #:style StyleAscii)])
    ;; +-----------+---------+-------+
    ;; | Item      |   Price | Stock |
    ;; +-----------+---------+-------+
    ;; | Green tea |    3.50 |    12 |
    ;; | Gaiwan    | 1240.00 |     2 |
    ;; | Kettle    |   89.90 |     0 |
    ;; +-----------+---------+-------+
    """)
  (reference """
    The same table as @code{tabular} makes of the columns these rows are
    made of. It is @code{table-row} on each row, with the layout that
    @code{table-widths} gives them; rows of different lengths are fine, a
    short one ending early.
    """))

(:doc Align
  (summary "Where a cell sits in its table column.")
  (case AlignLeft "Against the left edge, padded on the right. The default.")
  (case AlignRight "Against the right edge, padded on the left: what numbers want.")
  (case AlignCentre "In the middle, the odd column of padding on the right.")
  (case AlignOn "Every line of the column has the character in the same place, as a decimal point is in a column of numbers. A line without the character ends where the character would be. The part as a whole is aligned to the right, and so is the title over it.")
  (see tabular tabular/rows table-layout columnar)
  (example """
    (show [(tabular/rows [["tea" "3.5"] ["gaiwan" "1240"] ["kettle" "89.90"]]
                         #:align [AlignLeft (AlignOn #\.)])])
    ;; tea       3.5
    ;; gaiwan 1240
    ;; kettle   89.90
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{AlignOn} needs the widest part of a line before the character and
    the widest part from it on, so it is measured by the tables that see all
    their rows: @code{tabular} and @code{tabular/rows}. A table laid out a
    row at a time, and @code{columnar}, align such a column to the right.
    """))

(:doc TableStyle
  (summary "How a table's borders and rules are drawn.")
  (case StyleNone "No borders: the columns with a separator between them. The default.")
  (case StyleSimple "No borders, two spaces between the columns, and a line of ─ under the header.")
  (case StyleAscii "A box of +, - and |.")
  (case StyleLight "A box of light lines: ┌─┬─┐.")
  (case StyleHeavy "A box of heavy lines: ┏━┳━┓.")
  (case StyleDouble "A box of double lines: ╔═╦═╗.")
  (case StyleRounded "A box of light lines with round corners: ╭─┬─╮.")
  (case StyleMarkdown "A Markdown table, whose rule under the header says how each column is aligned.")
  (case StyleCustom "The pieces of a BorderChars.")
  (see tabular/rows tabular table-layout BorderChars TableRules)
  (example """
    (show [(tabular/rows [["tea" "3.50"] ["gaiwan" "1240.00"]]
                         #:titles ["Item" "Price"]
                         #:style StyleAscii
                         #:align [AlignLeft AlignRight])])
    ;; +--------+---------+
    ;; | Item   |   Price |
    ;; +--------+---------+
    ;; | tea    |    3.50 |
    ;; | gaiwan | 1240.00 |
    ;; +--------+---------+
    ;;
    ;; The other boxes are the same table drawn with other characters.
    """)
  (reference """
    A style with a box pads each cell with a space on each side, and pads
    every cell to its column, the last one too, so that the right edge is
    straight. A Markdown column is at least three wide, as its rule needs.
    """))

(:doc TableRules
  (summary "Which horizontal rules a table has inside its borders.")
  (case RulesHeader "A rule under the header. The default.")
  (case RulesEvery "A rule under the header and between every two rows. Markdown has none between rows.")
  (case RulesNone "No rules inside the table.")
  (see TableStyle tabular/rows))

(:doc ColumnWidth
  (summary "How wide a table column is.")
  (case WidthAuto "As wide as its widest line. The default.")
  (case WidthFixed "This many columns, at least one.")
  (case WidthAtLeast "As wide as its content, and at least this many columns.")
  (case WidthAtMost "As wide as its content, and at most this many columns.")
  (case WidthShare "A share of what the other columns and the borders leave of current-width: two columns of shares 1 and 2 take a third and two thirds of it.")
  (see tabular/rows Overflow current-width)
  (example """
    (with-fmt current-width 30
      [(tabular/rows [["a" "b" "c"]] #:style StyleAscii
                     #:widths [(WidthFixed 5) (WidthShare 1) (WidthShare 2)])])
    ;; +-------+-------+------------+
    ;; | a     | b     | c          |
    ;; +-------+-------+------------+
    """)
  (reference """
    A column narrower than its content shows the content as its
    @code{Overflow} says: pushed out of line, cut or wrapped. A width of no
    more than zero is one.
    """))

(:doc Overflow
  (summary "What a table cell wider than its column does.")
  (case OverflowPush "It pushes the rest of its row out of line. The default, and what a table without width rules does.")
  (case OverflowCut "It is cut to the column, on the side away from its alignment, with current-ellipsis in place of what is cut.")
  (case OverflowWrap "It is broken at its spaces onto more lines, and a word wider than the column is broken where it reaches the edge.")
  (see tabular/rows ColumnWidth current-width current-ellipsis)
  (example """
    (with-fmt current-width 40
      [(tabular/rows [["tea" "A green tea from the hills, picked in spring and rolled by hand."]]
                     #:titles ["Item" "Note"] #:style StyleAscii #:overflow OverflowWrap)])
    ;; +------+-------------------------------+
    ;; | Item | Note                          |
    ;; +------+-------------------------------+
    ;; | tea  | A green tea from the hills,   |
    ;; |      | picked in spring and rolled   |
    ;; |      | by hand.                      |
    ;; +------+-------------------------------+
    """)
  (reference """
    A table that cuts or wraps is made to fit @code{current-width}: while it
    is too wide, the widest column that may shrink loses a column. A column
    of a fixed width does not shrink, nor one at its @code{WidthAtLeast},
    nor one below one column. A column aligned on a character is neither
    cut nor wrapped, since the character would no longer line up.
    """))

(:doc BorderChars
  (summary "The pieces a table's borders are drawn with, for a style of your own.")
  (field top-left "The start of the line above the table.")
  (field top "Repeated across each column in the line above the table. Empty means there is no such line.")
  (field top-join "Where two columns meet in the line above the table.")
  (field top-right "The end of the line above the table.")
  (field left "Before the first cell of a row.")
  (field column "Between two cells of a row.")
  (field right "After the last cell of a row.")
  (field rule-left "The start of a rule.")
  (field rule "Repeated across each column in a rule. Empty means there are no rules.")
  (field rule-join "Where two columns meet in a rule.")
  (field rule-right "The end of a rule.")
  (field bottom-left "The start of the line under the table.")
  (field bottom "Repeated across each column in the line under the table. Empty means there is no such line.")
  (field bottom-join "Where two columns meet in the line under the table.")
  (field bottom-right "The end of the line under the table.")
  (field marks "Whether the rule under the header marks each column's alignment with colons, as Markdown's does.")
  (see TableStyle)
  (reference """
    A piece of a horizontal line is as wide as the piece of a row it is
    under: @code{top-left} as @code{left}, @code{top-join} as
    @code{column}, and so on. The built-in boxes put a space on each side of
    a cell, so their @code{left} is @code{"│ "} and their @code{top-left}
    is @code{"┌─"}. A table without @code{left} and @code{right} leaves out
    the empty cells at the end of a line and does not pad its last cell, as
    a table without borders does.
    """))

(:doc TableLayout
  (summary "The widths, alignments, separator and style of a table laid out a row at a time.")
  (field widths "The width of each column.")
  (field aligns "The alignment of each column, from the first; a column not given one is AlignLeft.")
  (field sep "Written between the columns, without borders.")
  (field style "How the borders are drawn.")
  (see table-layout table-row))

(:doc table-layout
  (summary "A layout for table-row: the width of each column, settled before any row is written.")
  (arg widths "The width of each column, in columns as current-string-width counts them.")
  (key sep "Written between the columns, without borders. A space unless given.")
  (key align "The alignment of each column, from the first; a column not given one is AlignLeft.")
  (key style "How the borders are drawn. StyleNone unless given.")
  (returns "A TableLayout.")
  (see table-row table-widths table-top table-rule table-bottom))

(:doc table-top
  (summary "The line above a table laid out a row at a time.")
  (arg layout "The table's layout.")
  (returns "A block: the line, or nothing for a style without one.")
  (see table-row table-rule table-bottom)
  (example """
    (def layout (table-layout [6 5] #:style StyleAscii #:align [AlignLeft AlignRight]))
    (show [(table-top layout) (table-row layout ["Item" "Price"]) (table-rule layout)
           (table-row layout ["tea" "3.50"]) (table-bottom layout)])
    ;; +--------+-------+
    ;; | Item   | Price |
    ;; +--------+-------+
    ;; | tea    |  3.50 |
    ;; +--------+-------+
    """))

(:doc table-rule
  (summary "A rule across a table laid out a row at a time: under its header, or between two rows.")
  (arg layout "The table's layout.")
  (returns "A block: the rule, or nothing for a style without rules.")
  (see table-top table-row table-bottom))

(:doc table-bottom
  (summary "The line under a table laid out a row at a time.")
  (arg layout "The table's layout.")
  (returns "A block: the line, or nothing for a style without one.")
  (see table-top table-row table-rule))

(:doc table-widths
  (summary "The width each column of some rows asks for: its widest cell.")
  (arg rows "The rows, each a vec of cells, a block per cell.")
  (returns "A vec with a width for every column any of the rows has.")
  (see table-layout tabular/rows)
  (example """
    (table-widths [["name" "median"] ["fib 20" "14.9 µs" "note"]])
    ;; [6 7 4]
    """))

(:doc table-row
  (summary "One row of a table, laid out to a layout fixed beforehand, so that it can be written as soon as it is known.")
  (arg layout "The widths, alignments and separator, from table-layout.")
  (arg row "The cells, a block each; a newline in a cell gives the row another line.")
  (returns "A block.")
  (see table-layout table-widths tabular/rows)
  (example """
    ;; Each result printed when it is measured, under a heading printed first.
    (def layout (table-layout [8 8] #:sep "  " #:align [AlignLeft AlignRight]))
    (show [(table-row layout ["name" "time"])])
    (loop (:for (Tuple name t) results)
          (:do (show [(table-row layout [name t])])))
    ;; name          time
    ;; sort        1.2 µs
    """)
  (reference """
    Every cell is padded to its column's width as its alignment says, and a
    left-aligned last cell is not padded at all. A cell wider than its column
    is not cut: it pushes the rest of its row to the right, and the rows
    after it are not affected. Cells past the columns of the layout are
    written as they are, with the separator before each. Empty cells at the
    end of a line are left out, separators and all. The row starts on a
    fresh line and ends with a newline.
    """))

(:doc Table
  (summary "A table written as a quoted literal: its options, its columns, and its rows.")
  (field options "The table's style, header, rules, width and overflow, each there or not.")
  (field columns "The columns, each with its title, alignment, width and kind.")
  (field rows "The rows, each written or spliced.")
  (see table-block show-table TableCell tabular/rows)
  (example """
    (: (Vec (Vec TableCell)))
    (def products
      [[(CellText "Green tea") (CellNum 3.5) (CellInt 12) (CellNum 0.4)]
       [(CellText "Gaiwan") (CellNum 1240.0) (CellInt 2) (CellNum 1.0)]])

    (show-table
     '(table (style ascii) header
             (column "Item")
             (column "Price" (align (on #\.)) (number (precision 2) (comma 3)))
             (column "Stock" (align right))
             (column "Share" (percent (precision 0)))
             (rows ,products)))
    ;; +-----------+----------+-------+-------+
    ;; | Item      |    Price | Stock | Share |
    ;; +-----------+----------+-------+-------+
    ;; | Green tea |     3.50 |    12 |   40% |
    ;; | Gaiwan    | 1,240.00 |     2 |  100% |
    ;; +-----------+----------+-------+-------+
    ;;
    ;; A column of (bar (max 100.0)) shows each number as a bar of its
    ;; share of the max, as the block bar does.

    (show-table '(table (style markdown) header (column "Key") (column "Value")
                        (rows (row "width" 78) (row "style" "markdown"))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The literal is @code{(table option ... column ... (rows row ...))}. The
    options come first, each at most once and in any order:
    @code{(style s)} with a @code{TableStyle} as its name, @code{rounded} or
    @code{markdown}; @code{header}, which makes the column titles a header
    row; @code{(rules r)} with a @code{TableRules}; @code{(width n)}, the
    width the table is fitted to in place of @code{current-width}; and
    @code{(overflow o)} with an @code{Overflow}.

    A column is @code{(column "Title" option ...)}, its options again each
    at most once: @code{(align a)} with an @code{Align}, as @code{right} or
    @code{(on #\.)}; @code{(width w)} with a @code{ColumnWidth}, as
    @code{12} or @code{(share 2)}; and a kind, which shows the column's
    numbers as one of this module's blocks: @code{(number (precision 2)
    (comma 3) signed)} as @code{numeric}, @code{bytes}, @code{(percent
    (precision 1))}, or @code{(bar (max 100.0) (width 10))}, a bar of the
    number's share of the max. A column of a kind other than a bar is
    aligned to the right unless it says otherwise. A cell that is not a
    number is shown as it is in any column.

    A row is written, @code{(row cell ...)}, a cell being a string or a
    number or a spliced value of one; or spliced, as a @code{(Vec
    TableCell)} for one row or a @code{(Vec (Vec TableCell))} for many. A
    spliced number has to have a type: a @code{def} of a bare literal does
    not say what it is.

    The literal is read where it is written, so a misspelled option, an
    option given twice, a style that is not one, a table with no columns
    and a splice of the wrong type are compile errors that say where.
    """))

(:doc TableCell
  (summary "A cell of a Table: what a written cell is, and what a spliced row holds.")
  (case CellText "Text, shown as it is.")
  (case CellInt "A whole number, shown as its column's kind says.")
  (case CellNum "A number, shown as its column's kind says.")
  (case CellBlocks "Blocks, shown as they are, as each shows them.")
  (see Table))

(:doc table-block
  (summary "A table literal as a block.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (returns "A block, laid out as tabular/rows lays out a table.")
  (see Table show-table tabular/rows))

(:doc show-table
  (summary "Writes a table literal to the current output port.")
  (arg t "The table.")
  (see Table table-block show))
